      "gap": "ED26-P15-CH-martingale-maximal-inequalities-C021: the historical and modelling claims of the source's Exercise 4.3.13 (PTE5 p. 234) are not supplied: Galton and Watson, who invented the process that bears their names, were interested in the survival of family names; each family has exactly three children whose sexes are determined by coin flips; in the 1800s only male children kept the family name, so following the male offspring gives p_0 = 1/8, p_1 = 3/8, p_2 = 3/8, p_3 = 1/8; and the exercise asks for the probability rho that the family name dies out when Z_0 = 1. thm:p15-branching-calculations (body 382-403) says only that the binomial family-name law is the source's; its computations and the two supplied limiting laws are otherwise complete.",
